#language: en
#utf-8

@all @xen_007 @git @accounts @accounts_ssh
Feature: SSH key accounts
  An SSH account uses an existing key file (its passphrase, if any, kept in the OS keychain), the
  SSH agent, or a new ed25519 key generated by Xenon into app data (owner-only, public key shown
  and, for a GitHub account, uploadable to GitHub). "Test connection" reads the repository with
  the account. The host key of the test server is trusted beforehand here; host_keys.feature
  covers first-use confirmation.

  Background:
    Given the keychain is available
    And the remote "acme/backend" has the file "api/xenon/workspace.yaml" with
      """
      name: Acme
      """
    And the "repo" variable is set to the ssh url of the remote "acme/backend"
    And the "ssh_host" variable is set to the git ssh server host
    And the "known_line" variable is set to the git ssh server known hosts line
    And the app data file "ssh/known_hosts" contains
      """
      {{known_line}}
      """

  @key_file
  Scenario: An existing key file protected by a passphrase
    Given an ssh key "work" is saved at the temp path "keys/work" with the passphrase "correct-horse-7"
    And the git ssh server authorizes the ssh key "work"
    When I call "GitAccountService.AddSSH" with
      """
      { "mode": "key_file", "key_path": "{{temp_root}}/keys/work", "passphrase": "correct-horse-7", "host": "{{ssh_host}}", "commit_name": "Luis", "commit_email": "luis@acme.test" }
      """
    Then the call should succeed
    And the response should contain the field "public_key" equal to "{{work_public_key}}"
    And the response should contain the field "account.kind" equal to "ssh"
    And the response should contain the field "account.ssh_mode" equal to "key_file"
    And the response should contain the field "account.has_passphrase" equal to "true"
    And the response should not contain "correct-horse-7"
    And I save the response field "account.id" as "account"
    And the keychain should contain an entry for the account "{{account}}"
    And no file under the app data folder should contain "correct-horse-7"
    When I call "GitAccountService.TestConnection" with
      """
      { "account_id": "{{account}}", "repository": "{{repo}}" }
      """
    Then the call should succeed
    And the response should contain the field "default_branch" equal to "main"

  @key_file
  Scenario: A key file without a passphrase stores nothing in the keychain
    Given an ssh key "plain" is saved at the temp path "keys/plain"
    When I call "GitAccountService.AddSSH" with
      """
      { "mode": "key_file", "key_path": "{{temp_root}}/keys/plain", "host": "{{ssh_host}}", "commit_name": "Luis", "commit_email": "luis@acme.test" }
      """
    Then the call should succeed
    And the response should contain the field "account.has_passphrase" equal to "false"
    And the keychain should contain 0 entries

  @key_file @invalid
  Scenario Outline: Key files that cannot be used - <case>
    Given an ssh key "work" is saved at the temp path "keys/work" with the passphrase "correct-horse-7"
    When I call "GitAccountService.AddSSH" with
      """
      { "mode": "key_file", "key_path": "{{temp_root}}/<path>", "passphrase": "<passphrase>", "host": "{{ssh_host}}", "commit_name": "Luis", "commit_email": "luis@acme.test" }
      """
    Then the call should fail with code "ACC-10400"
    And the keychain should contain 0 entries

    Examples:
      | case               | path         | passphrase      |
      | wrong passphrase   | keys/work    | not-the-one     |
      | missing passphrase | keys/work    |                 |
      | missing file       | keys/nothing | correct-horse-7 |

  @agent
  Scenario: The SSH agent
    Given an ssh key "agent" is saved at the temp path "keys/agent"
    And the ssh agent holds the ssh key "agent"
    And the git ssh server authorizes the ssh key "agent"
    When I call "GitAccountService.AddSSH" with
      """
      { "mode": "agent", "host": "{{ssh_host}}", "commit_name": "Luis", "commit_email": "luis@acme.test" }
      """
    Then the call should succeed
    And the response should contain the field "account.ssh_mode" equal to "agent"
    And I save the response field "account.id" as "account"
    When I call "GitAccountService.TestConnection" with
      """
      { "account_id": "{{account}}", "repository": "{{repo}}" }
      """
    Then the call should succeed

  @agent @invalid
  Scenario Outline: The SSH agent must be running with keys - <case>
    Given <agent>
    When I call "GitAccountService.AddSSH" with
      """
      { "mode": "agent", "host": "{{ssh_host}}", "commit_name": "Luis", "commit_email": "luis@acme.test" }
      """
    Then the call should fail with code "ACC-11400"

    Examples:
      | case        | agent                                 |
      | not running | the keychain is available             |
      | no keys     | the ssh agent is running without keys |

  @agent @invalid
  Scenario: A key the server does not accept fails the connection test with a next step
    Given an ssh key "stranger" is saved at the temp path "keys/stranger"
    And the ssh agent holds the ssh key "stranger"
    And I call "GitAccountService.AddSSH" with
      """
      { "mode": "agent", "host": "{{ssh_host}}", "commit_name": "Luis", "commit_email": "luis@acme.test" }
      """
    And I save the response field "account.id" as "account"
    When I call "GitAccountService.TestConnection" with
      """
      { "account_id": "{{account}}", "repository": "{{repo}}" }
      """
    Then the call should fail with code "GIT-11401"
    And the error should contain the field "error.error_details.0.attribute" equal to "next_step"

  @generated
  Scenario: A generated key is owner-only, shown to copy, uploadable to GitHub and deleted with the account
    Given the "api" variable is set to the mocked api url
    And the "XENON_GITHUB_API_URL" env var is set to "{{api}}"
    And the 1 "GET" request to "/user" returns status 200 with the following response
      """
      { "login": "luis", "id": 7 }
      """
    And the 1 "POST" request to "/user/keys" returns status 201 with the following response
      """
      { "id": 1 }
      """
    When I call "GitAccountService.AddSSH" with
      """
      { "mode": "generate", "host": "{{ssh_host}}", "commit_name": "Luis", "commit_email": "luis@acme.test" }
      """
    Then the call should succeed
    And the response should contain the field "public_key" matching "^ssh-ed25519 [A-Za-z0-9+/=]+ xenon$"
    And the response should contain the field "account.ssh_mode" equal to "generated"
    And I save the response field "account.id" as "ssh_account"
    And I save the response field "public_key" as "generated_key"
    And the app data file "ssh/xenon_{{ssh_account}}_ed25519" should exist
    And the app data path "ssh/xenon_{{ssh_account}}_ed25519" should have the permissions "600"
    And the app data file "ssh/xenon_{{ssh_account}}_ed25519.pub" should exist
    And the keychain should contain 0 entries
    Given the git ssh server authorizes the public key "{{generated_key}}"
    When I call "GitAccountService.TestConnection" with
      """
      { "account_id": "{{ssh_account}}", "repository": "{{repo}}" }
      """
    Then the call should succeed
    Given I call "GitAccountService.AddToken" with
      """
      { "provider": "github", "host": "github.com", "token": "ghp-upload-token", "commit_name": "", "commit_email": "" }
      """
    And I save the response field "id" as "github_account"
    When I call "GitAccountService.UploadPublicKey" with
      """
      { "ssh_account_id": "{{ssh_account}}", "github_account_id": "{{github_account}}", "title": "Xenon on my laptop" }
      """
    Then the call should succeed
    And the 1 "POST" request to "/user/keys" should have the body field "title" equal to "Xenon on my laptop"
    And the 1 "POST" request to "/user/keys" should have the body field "key" equal to "{{generated_key}}"
    And the 1 "POST" request to "/user/keys" should have the header "Authorization" equal to "Bearer ghp-upload-token"
    When I call "GitAccountService.Remove" with
      """
      { "id": "{{ssh_account}}" }
      """
    Then the call should succeed
    And the app data file "ssh/xenon_{{ssh_account}}_ed25519" should not exist
    And the app data file "ssh/xenon_{{ssh_account}}_ed25519.pub" should not exist

  @generated @invalid
  Scenario Outline: Upload needs an SSH account and a GitHub account - <case>
    Given the "api" variable is set to the mocked api url
    And the "XENON_GITHUB_API_URL" env var is set to "{{api}}"
    And the 1 "POST" request to "/user/keys" returns status 422 with the following response
      """
      { "message": "Validation Failed", "errors": [ { "message": "key is already in use" } ] }
      """
    And the git http server requires any username and the token "tok-gitlab"
    And I call "GitAccountService.AddSSH" with
      """
      { "mode": "generate", "host": "{{ssh_host}}", "commit_name": "Luis", "commit_email": "luis@acme.test" }
      """
    And I save the response field "account.id" as "ssh_account"
    And the "http_repo" variable is set to the http url of the remote "acme/backend"
    And the "http_host" variable is set to the git http server host
    And I call "GitAccountService.AddToken" with
      """
      { "provider": "gitlab", "host": "{{http_host}}", "token": "tok-gitlab", "repository": "{{http_repo}}", "commit_name": "Luis", "commit_email": "luis@acme.test" }
      """
    And I save the response field "id" as "gitlab_account"
    And the 1 "GET" request to "/user" returns status 200 with the following response
      """
      { "login": "luis", "id": 7 }
      """
    And I call "GitAccountService.AddToken" with
      """
      { "provider": "github", "host": "github.com", "token": "ghp-x", "commit_name": "", "commit_email": "" }
      """
    And I save the response field "id" as "github_account"
    When I call "GitAccountService.UploadPublicKey" with
      """
      { "ssh_account_id": "{{<ssh>}}", "github_account_id": "{{<github>}}", "title": "Xenon" }
      """
    Then the call should fail with code "<code>"

    Examples:
      | case                  | ssh            | github         | code      |
      | not a github account  | ssh_account    | gitlab_account | ACC-12400 |
      | not an ssh account    | gitlab_account | github_account | ACC-12400 |
      | key refused by GitHub | ssh_account    | github_account | ACC-13422 |
